--- FIRST RUN: the harness read part of the right line -------------------------

[E] scored FAIL while the gate under it was working. The refusal fired and
printed both of its lines; the harness's own filter -

    NARRATIVE='narrative|Current state|As of|newest block|second column'

- matched the second line and not the first, because
`docs/discussion-log.md says <date>, the newest session is <date>` contains none
of those words. So the variant showed only

        session-close: the narrative is behind the cursor, which is how Phase 16b closed

and scored it as a miss. Nothing was wrong with the check; the instrument read
part of the right line. That is the same shape as the thing this whole phase is
about, one layer further out, and it is why the filter now carries a paragraph
instead of a wider regex typed in silence.

    break-narrative-phase: 7 of 8 variants behaved as written.
    EXIT=1

The other seven behaved identically in both runs.

--- WHAT EACH VARIANT IS FOR ---------------------------------------------------

[A] control        the tree as committed
[B] the defect     Phase 28's block missing, Phase 27's on top, same date
[C] no phase       the block is present and dated right, parenthesis removed
[D] no column      the newest INDEX row's phase column emptied
[E] regression     the date check must still fire, and fire as a date
[F] no block       no `**As of` line anywhere in the file
[G] positive       a title long enough to wrap must NOT make the check go quiet.
                   The 2026-08-08 fix in session-close.sh exists because a
                   pattern that demanded the whole shape stopped matching when
                   the shape grew; this reads only the token after `(`.
[H] control        green again, after every mutation was restored

--- THE DEVBOX RUN, AND WHAT IT FOUND: 2 OF 8 -------------------------------

Run on the devbox after `git am`, with Phase 29's own Current state block now at
the top of the file:

    === [A] control: the tree as committed ===
    FAIL  the narrative names the newest phase and the check says which
          expected to match: narrative 2026-08-11 \(28\), matching the newest session
            narrative 2026-08-12 (29), matching the newest session
    ...
    break-narrative-phase: 2 of 8 variants behaved as written.

THE SCRIPT WAS FROZEN TO THE PHASE THAT WROTE IT. `28` and `2026-08-11` were
literals. In the sandbox that was invisible, because Phase 29's block did not
exist yet and the newest block WAS 28's. The moment the closing patch landed,
every mutation went into a block BELOW the one the check reads, the check
answered `phase 29` each time, and the harness scored six working refusals as
failures. [D] and [F] survived, being the only two variants that never name a
phase.

It failed loudly, which is the good outcome, and it is the wrong failure to be
satisfied with: the entire reason a break script is committed rather than typed
into a session is that a LATER session can re-run it. This one could not have
been. Third time in one phase that an instrument was pointed at the wrong part of
the right thing - after the log filter that read one line of two, and the walk
that stopped at its own ceiling.

Fixed by deriving: the newest date and phase come from the last INDEX row, the
phase of the block underneath comes from the file's second `**As of` line, and
[B] now FORCES the previous block's date to the newest session's rather than
assuming two consecutive phases closed on the same day - which is exactly the
condition the defect needs and is not always true. Two refusals were added for
the anchors themselves: unreadable, and the two newest blocks naming the same
phase (which would mutate one and judge the other).

    anchors: newest session 2026-08-12 phase 29; the block under it is phase 28
    break-narrative-phase: 8 of 8 variants behaved as written.

AND THE FIX WAS PROVED THE ONLY WAY THAT MEANS ANYTHING - by running it in the
future. A synthetic `phase 30` block and INDEX row were COMMITTED (uncommitted
would prove nothing: `restore` is `git checkout`, and the first variant would
have reverted the plant - which is how the first attempt at this probe scored
5 of 8 and blamed the script):

    anchors: newest session 2026-08-13 phase 30; the block under it is phase 29
    break-narrative-phase: 8 of 8 variants behaved as written.

The synthetic commit was discarded.
